---
import Section from './Section.astro';
import { site } from '../config';

const spec = [
  {
    term: 'Delivery',
    detail: 'A Rust library with a C interface, so an integrator links it into an existing firmware.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Carries',
    detail:
      'The on-media format, the state contract, the media channels, and the C surface.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Record kinds',
    detail: 'Facts, intents, and hints.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Portability',
    detail:
      'The format fixes the layout, the keys, the encoding, and how identifiers are derived, so a volume moves between machines without a conversion step.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Range',
    detail:
      'There is no operating system dependency and no index to size, so the library is not bounded by the small end. The build that runs on a bare microcontroller builds for a server or a data center machine.',
  },
];
---

<Section
  id="product"
  label="Product"
  title="A Rust library with a C interface"
  lede="Ktema ships to firmware teams as something they link, not as something they port."
>
  <dl class="spec">
    {
      spec.map((row) => (
        <>
          <dt>{row.term}</dt>
          <dd>{row.detail}</dd>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <div>
    <h3>Rem, the reference product</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Rem is an offline spatial AI memory that runs the engine over a real FAT32 medium from a
        desktop application, a CLI, and an MCP client. It exercises the engine against real
        media, which is how the format is validated outside the firmware target.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .link-row {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 14px;
  }
</style>
